---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { identityConfig } from "../lib/env.mjs";

// The return leg, and only the return leg.
//
// Premise, restated because a premise stated in one file gets violated in every
// other one: **plurality is the product; the registry never designates,
// consumers pin, visibly.** Signing in confers no standing. Nothing on this page
// ranks, scores, approves or filters anything, there is no queue and there is
// nothing to approve: the namespace is the handle the provider reported, so
// there is no question for a reviewer to answer.
//
// **The submission form left this page on 2026-09-20 and went to `/submit/`.**
// It was here because the session died with the tab, so the only page that could
// know who was signed in was the one that had just signed them in. The session
// persists now, and the cost of leaving them joined was being paid in the bar:
// **Add artifact** pointed here, so pressing it during a return leg reloaded the
// page and discarded the exchange in progress. What stays is what the page's own
// name says: the redirect comes back here, the reading of what the provider said
// happens here, and the capture is downloaded here.
//
// **This page reverses a property recorded twice, deliberately rather than as a
// side effect.** `astro.config.mjs` says a build that emits files cannot drift
// into being a public surface the way a running process can, and that is still
// true of this build: `output: "static"`, no adapter, no route, no origin here
// that receives anything. What this page adds is a client. It sends data to two
// origins that are not this one, the Supabase project and Hugging Face, on the
// reader's instruction and with the reader's own credentials. `DECISIONS.md`
// 2026-09-20 records the reversal and what it costs, and `tests/test_intake.py`
// was rewritten around the structural criterion rather than deleted: the
// question is now where a request is aimed, not whether one exists.
//
// **It is also the window the upload offer comes back through.** The redirect
// URL registered with the identity service is this page, so the permission
// window `/submit/` opens lands here, and the branch at the bottom relays the
// code to whoever opened it and closes. That window holds no verifier and makes
// no exchange: the page that opened it does both. Adding `/submit/` to the
// allowed redirect URLs would be a console change only the account holder can
// make, and nothing here needs one.
//
// **One sign-in returns two credentials and they have opposite lifetimes.**
//
// - **The Supabase session**, `access_token` with its `refresh_token`, is what
//   the insert policy on `pending_submission` scopes to putting one row in as
//   yourself. It reads nobody else's rows and there is no update or delete
//   policy at all. It is written to `localStorage` under one key by
//   `astro/src/lib/held.mjs`, which is the only thing in this project that
//   writes it, and is restored and renewed by both pages that need it.
// - **The Hugging Face `provider_token`** can create and write repositories in
//   somebody's own namespace once `contribute-repos` is granted. It is written
//   nowhere, in any form. It lives in one local variable for the length of one
//   call and the frame ends. Hugging Face returns it on the sign-in itself and
//   never on a renewal, verified against supabase/auth v2.197.0 on 2026-09-20,
//   so keeping it would buy nothing past its expiry and would put a credential
//   that reaches an account somewhere that outlives the reason it was asked for.
//
// The PKCE verifier is still in `sessionStorage`, because a verifier has to
// survive a navigation by definition, and it is removed the moment it is used.
//
// **What a restored session cannot do.** Read a membership. That needs the
// provider token, which is gone, so this page shows the reading only on the
// load that did the sign-in and says so rather than showing yesterday's answer
// as today's.
//
// **Sign out in the bar deletes the key.** That ends the session in this
// browser and tells nobody, so the session is not revoked at the identity
// service and lapses there on its own. The bar says both.
//
// **The copy is drafted, not dictated.** Structure and accuracy are mine; every
// sentence a reader sees is the author's to rewrite.

const { url: supabaseUrl, key: supabaseKey, configured } = identityConfig();

// Addressed rather than enumerated. A custom OIDC provider in Supabase is named
// `custom:<name>`, and this is the one that is configured. Nothing anywhere
// compares it against a set of names.
const PROVIDER = "custom:huggingface";

// Fetched rather than remembered: every endpoint the browser talks to at the
// identity provider comes out of the provider's own discovery document at the
// moment of the capture, and the document's `issuer` and `userinfo_endpoint`
// are written into the capture, so the evidence says where it came from.
const DISCOVERY = "https://huggingface.co/.well-known/openid-configuration";
---

<Base title="Signed in" trail={[{ text: "controlbun", href: "/" }]} synthetic="none">
  <h1 slot="heading">Signed in</h1>

  <div
    id="handshake"
    data-supabase-url={supabaseUrl}
    data-supabase-key={supabaseKey}
    data-provider={PROVIDER}
    data-discovery={DISCOVERY}
  >
  </div>

  {!configured && (
    <p class="banner">
      <strong>This build was made without an identity endpoint.</strong> There is
      no button on this page, because a button that cannot work is worse than no
      button. The endpoint is read at build time from a file this repository does
      not carry.
    </p>
  )}

  <p class="lede">
    Identity here is Hugging Face's. This registry holds no password and has
    nothing to sign up for; signing in makes a record at the identity service
    behind this site, which is how a submission is known to be yours and is the
    whole of what an account means here. Reading this site needs none of it:
    browsing, comparing, following a pin and downloading an artifact are
    anonymous and stay that way.
  </p>

  {configured && (
    <section id="start" hidden>
      <p>
        Hugging Face will ask whether to let this registry read who you are: your
        opaque subject id, the handle you hold at this moment, and the
        organizations it says you belong to. It is not asked for write access to
        anything, and it is not asked for your email.
      </p>
      <p>
        <button type="button" id="begin" class="go">Sign in with Hugging Face</button>
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Signing in writes no submission and publishes nothing. It leaves one
        session in this browser, so coming back later does not ask again, and
        Sign out in the bar at the top deletes it. Whether to send anything is a
        separate press, on a separate page, after you have read what it would
        send.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <p id="working" hidden class="note">Reading what Hugging Face says about this
    account.</p>

  <p id="failed" hidden class="banner"></p>

  {/* A session came back off this browser. */}
  <p id="remembered" hidden class="banner"></p>

  {/* And the other answer: there was a session and it could not be renewed.
      Its own state with its own words, which is what the rest of this project
      does with an absent eval and an unclaimed namespace. */}
  <p id="stale" hidden class="banner"></p>

  <section id="who" hidden>
    <h2>What Hugging Face said, and when it said it</h2>
    <dl class="said">
      <dt>handle</dt><dd id="who-handle"></dd>
      <dt>subject</dt><dd id="who-sub"></dd>
      <dt>read at</dt><dd id="who-when"></dd>
    </dl>
    <div id="who-orgs"></div>
    <p class="note">
      Nothing rechecks any of this, so nothing here will ever claim you belong to
      an organization now. People join and leave; a membership read once and held
      forever goes stale quietly while still looking like a fact. Checking again
      means signing in again, which is a second reading beside the first rather
      than over it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <button type="button" id="keep-capture">Download this reading</button>
      <span class="note">
        One JSON file. It is the evidence a namespace claim is made from, and it
        carries no token.
      </span>
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      That sign-in returned two credentials and they are kept differently. The
      session for this registry, which can put one row in the holding table as
      you and can read, change and delete nothing else, is <strong>kept in this
      browser</strong> under one key, with your handle and your subject beside
      it, so a visit tomorrow is not a second sign-in and the bar at the top can
      offer <strong>Add artifact</strong> on a page this one did not render. The
      Hugging Face token, which could create and write repositories in your
      account if you take the upload offer when you submit, is <strong>kept
      nowhere at all</strong>: it is held in one variable for the length of that
      one call and Hugging Face never returns it on a renewal, so keeping it
      would buy nothing and risk your account. <strong>Sign out</strong> in the
      bar deletes the session and the handle from this browser. It tells nobody,
      so the session is not revoked at the identity service and lapses there on
      its own, and it does not end your Hugging Face session.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="onward" hidden>
    <h2>Your namespace is your handle, and there is no field for it</h2>
    <p>
      Anything you submit is published under <strong id="namespace-shown"></strong>,
      the handle Hugging Face reported just now, and the database writes it from
      your session rather than from anything you type. It is shown to you, not
      offered: no box, and no default to type over.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      What that makes impossible, stated because a rule that cannot name its cost
      has not been thought through: publishing under a pseudonym, under a lab name
      you hold no account for, or into one namespace shared by several people who
      each sign in as themselves. The last is a real shape, and today the answer
      is that a group claims a namespace rather than submits into one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/submit/" class="go">Submit a direction</a>{" "}
      <span class="note">
        Which is a separate page and a separate press. Nothing has been sent by
        signing in, and nothing is sent until you read the record on that page
        and press send.
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>What this page is not</h2>
  <p class="note">
    It is not an account. It is not a queue, and nothing here is reviewed,
    ranked, counted or ordered. It keeps no cookie. The one thing it writes is
    the session for this registry, which stays in this browser under one key so a
    return visit is not a second sign-in and which Sign out deletes. The Hugging
    Face token is the thing it never writes down anywhere, because that one
    reaches your account rather than this site. A namespace takes any number of
    claimants, and two people claiming one name render as two claims with their
    dates rather than as a conflict anybody here resolves.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/sign-in/">What signing in is for, and what a claim is</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .said { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap: 0.2rem 1rem; }
  .said dt { font-weight: 600; }
  .said dd { margin: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>

<script>
  import {
    authorizeUrl,
    captureFrom,
    confirmations,
    downloadName,
    nowStamp,
    pkcePair,
    READ_SCOPES,
    signerFrom,
    Refused,
  } from "../lib/handshake.mjs";
  import { discovery, exchange, HubError, userinfo } from "../lib/hub.mjs";
  import { keep, resume, staleSaid } from "../lib/held.mjs";

  const config = document.getElementById("handshake").dataset;
  const SUPABASE = config.supabaseUrl;
  const KEY = config.supabaseKey;
  const RETURN_TO = new URL("/signed-in/", location.origin).href;

  // The one thing that survives a navigation, because a PKCE verifier has to by
  // definition. Removed the moment it is used, and it is not a credential for
  // anything on its own: without the authorization code it exchanges for
  // nothing.
  const VERIFIER = "controlbun.pkce";

  const show = (id, on = true) => {
    const node = document.getElementById(id);
    if (node) node.hidden = !on;
  };
  const say = (id, text) => {
    const node = document.getElementById(id);
    if (node) node.textContent = text;
  };

  function stop(problem) {
    show("working", false);
    show("failed", true);
    say(
      "failed",
      problem instanceof Refused || problem instanceof HubError
        ? problem.message
        : `${problem}`,
    );
  }

  function download(name, text) {
    const url = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([text], { type: "application/json" }));
    const link = document.createElement("a");
    link.href = url;
    link.download = name;
    link.click();
    URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // Leg one: leaving.

  async function begin() {
    const { verifier, challenge } = await pkcePair();
    sessionStorage.setItem(VERIFIER, verifier);
    location.assign(authorizeUrl(SUPABASE, {
      provider: config.provider,
      redirectTo: RETURN_TO,
      challenge,
      scopes: READ_SCOPES.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean),
    }));
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // Leg two: arriving.

  /**
   * Code in, capture out, and the session kept on the way through.
   *
   * The two tokens the exchange returns go different ways here, which is the
   * whole of the arrangement. The Supabase session is written the moment it
   * exists, before the reading below, because the exchange is what made it and
   * a userinfo failure is a different fact about a different service: somebody
   * who hits one can reload and carry on rather than signing in twice. The
   * Hugging Face token goes into `userinfo` and into no variable that outlives
   * this call.
   */
  async function capture(code) {
    if (!SUPABASE || !KEY) {
      throw new Refused(
        "this build was made without an identity endpoint, so there is " +
          "nowhere to exchange this code. That is a fact about how the site " +
          "was built rather than about your sign-in.",
      );
    }
    const verifier = sessionStorage.getItem(VERIFIER);
    sessionStorage.removeItem(VERIFIER);
    if (!verifier) {
      throw new Refused(
        "this tab has no verifier to exchange the code against, so the " +
          "sign-in cannot be completed here. That happens when the link was " +
          "opened in a different tab from the one that started it. Start again " +
          "from this page.",
      );
    }
    const session = await exchange(SUPABASE, KEY, { code, verifier });
    keep(session, { provider: config.provider });
    const token = session.provider_token;
    if (!token) {
      throw new Refused(
        "the session carried no provider token, so there was nothing to call " +
          "the userinfo endpoint with and no membership was read. Hugging Face " +
          "returns it on the sign-in itself and never on a refresh, so the fix " +
          "is to sign in again rather than to retry.",
      );
    }
    const document_ = await discovery(config.discovery);
    const said = await userinfo(document_.userinfo_endpoint, token);
    return captureFrom(said, {
      provider: config.provider,
      issuer: document_.issuer,
      endpoint: document_.userinfo_endpoint,
      capturedAt: nowStamp(),
    });
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // What the page shows.

  // The membership reading, when this load is the one that did the sign-in.
  // Null on a restored session, because reading it needs the provider token and
  // no browser here keeps one.
  let held = null;

  function render(entry) {
    held = entry;
    say("who-handle", entry.preferred_username ?? "the provider returned none");
    say("who-sub", entry.sub);
    say("who-when", entry.captured_at);
    say("namespace-shown", entry.preferred_username ?? "");

    const orgs = document.getElementById("who-orgs");
    orgs.textContent = "";
    if (entry.orgs === null) {
      const note = document.createElement("p");
      note.className = "note";
      note.textContent = entry.orgs_absence;
      orgs.append(note);
    } else if (entry.orgs.length === 0) {
      const note = document.createElement("p");
      note.textContent = `No organization, as of ${entry.captured_at}. That is an answer, not a blank.`;
      orgs.append(note);
    } else {
      const list = document.createElement("ul");
      for (const line of confirmations(entry)) {
        const item = document.createElement("li");
        item.textContent = line;
        list.append(item);
      }
      orgs.append(list);
    }

    show("start", false);
    show("working", false);
    show("remembered", false);
    show("stale", false);
    show("who", true);
    show("onward", Boolean(entry.preferred_username));
  }

  /**
   * A session came back off this browser, so there is no reading to show.
   *
   * The namespace and the way on are all of it. `#who` stays hidden rather than
   * showing the membership this browser saw last time: a membership read once
   * and held forever goes stale quietly while still looking like a fact, which
   * is the sentence already under that section, and it applies hardest to a
   * reading nothing here even re-fetched.
   */
  function resumed(kept, { renewed }) {
    const signer = signerFrom(kept);
    say("namespace-shown", signer.preferred_username ?? "");
    show("working", false);
    show("start", false);
    show("stale", false);
    show("onward", Boolean(signer.preferred_username));
    show("remembered", true);
    say(
      "remembered",
      `This browser kept the session from a sign-in on ${kept.recorded_at}, ` +
        "so Hugging Face was not asked again. " +
        (renewed
          ? "Its access token had expired and the identity service issued a " +
            "new one just now. "
          : "Its access token has not expired yet. ") +
        "What came back is the session for this registry, which is enough to " +
        "send a submission as you and reaches nothing in your Hugging Face " +
        "account. What did not come back is the reading of what Hugging Face " +
        "says about you, because that needs a token this browser keeps " +
        "nowhere; signing in again takes a fresh reading beside the old one " +
        "rather than over it. Sign out in the bar deletes the session and the " +
        "handle from this browser, tells nobody, and does not end your " +
        "Hugging Face session.",
    );
  }

  /**
   * A plain visit: restore, renew if the access token is spent, say which.
   *
   * Only the two pages with business for the endpoint renew. The bar is on
   * every page and renewing there would put the identity endpoint and the
   * publishable key on every page, which `tests/test_signed_in_page.py` holds
   * to the pages with business for one. So the bar answers off what the record
   * says it can do, and the pages that can ask, ask.
   */
  async function arrive() {
    if (!SUPABASE || !KEY) return;
    show("working", true);
    say("working", "Reading the session this browser kept.");
    const answer = await resume(SUPABASE, KEY, { provider: config.provider });
    show("working", false);
    if (answer.state === "none") {
      show("start", true);
      return;
    }
    if (answer.state === "stale") {
      // A refusal is a state and not an error: the reader did nothing wrong
      // and there is one thing to do about it. So the session is already gone,
      // the bar drops back to Sign in on the same frame, and the button that
      // starts a fresh one is on the screen under a sentence saying why it is
      // there.
      show("remembered", false);
      show("stale", true);
      say("stale", staleSaid(answer.problem));
      show("start", true);
      return;
    }
    resumed(answer.held, { renewed: answer.state === "renewed" });
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // Wiring.

  const begun = document.getElementById("begin");
  if (begun) {
    begun.addEventListener("click", () => {
      begin().catch(stop);
    });
  }

  document.getElementById("keep-capture").addEventListener("click", () => {
    if (!held) return;
    download(
      downloadName("capture", held.preferred_username, held.captured_at),
      JSON.stringify(held, null, 2),
    );
  });

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // Which of the three things this load is.

  const code = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("code");
  const failure = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("error_description");

  if (window.opener && (code || failure)) {
    // A permission window, opened by `/submit/`. It relays and closes, and does
    // nothing else: the page that opened it holds the verifier and makes the
    // exchange.
    //
    // `code || failure` as well as the opener, so a tab somebody opened some
    // other way does not close itself the moment it loads. A permission window
    // always arrives carrying one or the other; a plain visit carries neither.
    //
    // Detected on `window.opener` rather than on `window.name`, which is what
    // this was first written against. Chrome clears `window.name` when a tab
    // navigates to a cross-origin document, and every hop of an authorization
    // is cross-origin, so the name would be gone by the time the browser
    // arrived back here. An opener is set by `window.open` and by nothing this
    // site does: its own links are plain anchors in the same tab, and a
    // ctrl-click or a `target=_blank` gets a null opener in every current
    // browser.
    window.opener.postMessage(
      { controlbun: "scope", code, error: failure },
      location.origin,
    );
    window.close();
  } else if (failure) {
    stop(new Refused(`Hugging Face stopped the sign-in: ${failure}`));
  } else if (code) {
    show("working", true);
    // The code is taken out of the address bar before anything else, so it is
    // not in the URL a reader copies, shares or leaves in history.
    history.replaceState(null, "", location.pathname);
    capture(code).then(render).catch(stop);
  } else {
    // A plain visit, which is what Sign in in the bar produces. Somebody
    // arriving that way has the session this browser kept, or nothing.
    arrive().catch(stop);
  }
</script>
